  "text": "The line segment $P_1P_2$ is perpendicular to the line passing through the centers of the circles $C_1$ and $C_2$. The centers of the circles $C_1$ and $C_2$ are $(1, 2)$ and $(-1, 3)$, respectively. The slope of the line passing through these two points is $\\frac{3-2}{-1-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$. Therefore, the slope of the line $P_1P_2$ is the negative reciprocal of this slope, which is $2$.\n\nNow, we work inwards. The center of circle $C_1$, $(1, 2)$ is at a distance of $1$ unit from the line $P_1P_2$, and the center of circle $C_2$, $(-1, 3)$ is at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ units from the line $P_1P_2$. The equation of the line $P_1P_2$ is:\n\\[y - 2 = 2( x - 1),\\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[y = 2x.\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line that is tangent to $C_1$ at a point $P_1$ and tangent to $C_2$ at a point $P_2$ is:\n\\[2x - y + 2 = 0.\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\boxed{2x - y + 2 = 0}$.",
